s as observed in experimental analogs.\"\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\n                \"rxn_012\": 0.3\n            },\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.5,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": 0.6,\n            \"environmental_progression\": 0.95,\n            \"target_achievement\": 0.7,\n            \"overall_confidence\": 0.62,\n            \"feedback\": \"The direct atmospheric discharge route does produce glutamate from simple gases (Ring et al., 1972; Schlesinger & Miller, 1983). However, rxn_012 does not conserve atoms (multiple C, N, O; see balance check) and thus is infeasible on conservation grounds. The 1-step depiction omits full intermediate network and co-products known from experiments.\",\n            \"approved\": false,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\n                \"Expand rxn_012 to a more explicit multistep network or at minimum balance overall carbon, nitrogen and oxygen accounting.\",\n                \"Include canonical byproducts from atmospheric spark chemistry (e.g., other amino acids, CO2, H2) if possible.\"\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\n                \"rxn_015\": 0.3,\n                \"rxn_016\": 0.5,\n                \"rxn_017\": 0.3,\n                \"rxn_018\": 0.3,\n                \"rxn_019\": 0.5\n            },\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.4,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": 0.7,\n            \"environmental_progression\": 0.95,\n            \"target_achievement\": 0.7,\n            \"overall_confidence\": 0.57,\n            \"feedback\": \"The acrolein\u2013cyanide\u2013Strecker route is chemically plausible (Van Trump & Miller, 1972; Fern\u00e1ndez-Garc\u00eda et al., 2017), but as written, most steps (rxn_015, rxn_017, rxn_018) violate atom conservation (imbalanced N, C, O). This strongly compromises feasibility. Concentration requirements are extreme but acknowledged in the literature. Mechanistic sequence is correct but stoichiometry is not.\",\n            \"approved\": false,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\n                \"Rewrite reactions to correctly balance all elements at each step.\",\n                \"Include all expected byproducts (water, ammonia, CO, CO2, etc.) and more fully annotate intermediates with correct formulas.\"\n            ]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": 0.68,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"The network excels in covering all known experimentally accessible prebiotic routes to glutamate, aligning each step and environment with the literature. Mechanistically, nearly every class of route is represented (reductive amination, cyanide/hydantoin, Strecker, transamination, and atmospheric/impact pathways). However, the network is fatally compromised by consistent and pervasive failures of atomic/charge balance throughout its reaction nodes: 18 of 20 reactions are unbalanceable as written, and the NADH-mediated reaction cannot even be checked due to an unparsable formula. This is a critical error\u2014legitimate prebiotic network proposals must explicitly account for all atoms and charges in every transformation. While the sequence and overall strategy are strongly literature-aligned, the chemical feasibility score suffers not from lack of experimental precedent but from implementation mistakes (incorrect stoichiometry, untracked byproducts, or under-defined intermediates). The most direct remedy is to revisit every reaction, rewriting each for strict mass/charge balance, and to expand ambiguous one-step conversions into more granular, experimentally motivated steps. If all network steps were reformulated to conserve mass/charge, several routes (especially reductive amination, ZnS photochemistry, and transamination) would qualify as viable at the 0.8\u20130.9 confidence level or higher.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.5, "rxn_020": 0.8}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.6, "pathway_coherence": 0.85, "environmental_progression": 0.95, "target_achievement": 0.8, "overall_confidence": 0.72, "feedback": "This pathway captures the experimentally supported iron-sulfide-mediated reductive amination of alpha-KG (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 2003; Kitadai et al., 2019). The upstream alpha-KG generation from pyruvate/glyoxylate is supported by Fe-driven networks (Muchowska et al., 2019). However, the main transformation (rxn_001) is unbalanced with an excess oxygen atom, and cannot be verified balanced per the charge/atom balance check. This is a direct conservation law violation. Reaction mechanisms and conditions are sound, but all steps must be reformulated to restore mass balance. Alpha-KG must not be treated as a free C5 input. Otherwise, the integration of the iron-mineral chemistry is literature-aligned.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001"], "suggestions": ["Reformulate rxn_001 to ensure atom/charge balance, possibly include byproducts like CO2 or H2 as appropriate per cited experiments.", "Explicitly quantify H2 or other reductants used in the mineral-mediated reduction, in stoichiometrically correct numbers."]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_002": 0.5, "rxn_020": 0.8}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.6, "pathway_coherence": 0.85, "environmental_progression": 0.95, "target_achievement": 0.8, "overall_confidence": 0.72, "feedback": "The H2/Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-KG is well documented (Kaur et al., 2024) and uses geologically plausible reductants. The description aligns with literature for hydrothermal/alkaline conditions. However, rxn_002 is unbalanced with an excess oxygen atom, so mass conservation is violated. Validating Ni or meteorit