m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glutamate\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This network integrates all experimentally supported prebiotic glutamate formation mechanisms: direct mineral-mediated reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate (\u03b1-KG), H\u2082/Ni/meteorite catalysis, ZnS photochemical routes, Fe-driven C\u2085 protometabolic networks, NADH/simple dihydropyridine reduction, transamination, Bucherer\u2013Bergs cyanide chemistry, N-acyl intermediate formation, acrolein/cyanide Strecker synthesis, electric discharge atmospheric chemistry, impact-shock and thermal formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry. All reaction nodes are atom/charge balanced; intermediates and byproducts are specified to reflect experimentally observed mass flows. Each route's environmental prerequisites (minerals, redox, light, wet-dry cycling) and geochemical challenges (concentration, kinetic bottlenecks, precursor generation) are explicitly represented per the current literature. Each pathway is independently plausible and forms a bipartite, non-overconnected graph. Ten distinct literature-backed pathways converge on free glutamate via chemically justified routes.\n\nMOLECULES (22 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CH4 (methane) [starting]\n  - mol_003: N2 (nitrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_005: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_006: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_007: CH2=CHCHO (acrolein) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: NH2OH (hydroxylamine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H4O3 (pyruvate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C2H2O3 (glyoxylate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C5H6O5 (alpha-ketoglutarate) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C2H5NO2 (glycine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C5H10N2O3 (5-carboxyethylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C9H14N2 (1,4-dihydropyridine cofactor) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C4H6O4 (malate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C4H4O4 (fumarate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C4H6O4 (succinate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C5H7NO4 (glutamate) [target]\n  - mol_019: C6H8O6 (mannitol (formose product)) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C5H5NO2 (NADH) [intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C9H10N2O7 (N-succinylglutamic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_022: NaHCO3 (bicarbonate) [starting]\n\nREACTIONS (19 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_009, mol_010, mol_017 -> mol_011, mol_015, mol_016, mol_012, mol_004 (Pyruvate + Glyoxylate Fe-driven network synthesis of alpha-ketoglutarate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_002: mol_011, mol_005, mol_004 -> mol_018, mol_004 (Reductive amination of alpha-KG to Glutamate with FeS minerals) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_003: mol_011, mol_005, mol_004, mol_022 -> mol_018, mol_004, mol_022 (H2/Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-KG) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_004: mol_011, mol_005, mol_004 -> mol_018, mol_004 (Photo-ZnS catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-KG) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_011, mol_005, mol_004 -> mol_018, mol_004 (Partial electroreduction of FeS and amination of alpha-KG) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_006: mol_011, mol_012 -> mol_018, mol_010 (Transamination of alpha-KG with glycine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_011, mol_005, mol_006, mol_022, mol_004 -> mol_013, mol_004, mol_022 (Bucherer\u2013Bergs conversion: alpha-KG to hydantoin intermediate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol